    fullDescription: "The Musée de Marrakech occupies the Dar Menebhi, a lavish palace built at the turn of the twentieth century for Mehdi Menebhi, a defence minister under Sultan Abdelaziz, and restored in the 1990s by the Omar Benjelloun Foundation to serve as a museum and cultural centre. The building itself is the star attraction: its vast central courtyard, once open to the sky and now roofed in glass and metalwork, is dominated by an enormous, intricately worked brass lamp-chandelier suspended overhead, a much-photographed showpiece surrounded by walls of zellige tile, carved stucco and painted cedar. Off the courtyard lie the former private hammam, with its domed, star-punctured ceiling that scatters points of light, and a series of reception rooms now used as galleries. The collection ranges across Moroccan and Judeo-Moroccan art, historic Korans and calligraphy, ceramics, coins, textiles and traditional weaponry, alongside rotating displays of contemporary Moroccan painting, offering a broad if uneven survey of the country's material culture. It is a comfortable, shaded, partly air-conditioned stop in the heart of the northern medina, and its location on Place Ben Youssef makes it easy to combine with the neighbouring Ben Youssef Madrasa and the tiny, ancient Almoravid Koubba — the only surviving Almoravid building in the city, whose domed ablutions structure a combined ticket usually includes. Allow forty-five minutes to an hour, spend most of it admiring the courtyard and the domed hammam, and enjoy a cultural pause between souk forays in one of the medina's grandest interiors.",
