    fullDescription: "The Menara Gardens are among the oldest and most iconic green spaces in Marrakech, laid out by the Almohad dynasty in the twelfth century as a working agricultural estate and pleasure garden, and still functioning much as they did eight centuries ago. The gardens are essentially a vast olive grove — hundreds of hectares of silvery trees irrigated by a sophisticated system of underground channels (khettaras) that carry snowmelt down from the High Atlas — organised around an enormous central rainwater basin. Beside this large rectangular reflecting pool stands the small green-tiled pavilion, a nineteenth-century Saadian-era summer house whose image, mirrored in the water with the snow-capped Atlas rising behind, is one of the most reproduced views of Marrakech and a staple of tourist posters. The scene is at its most beautiful in the late afternoon and at sunset, when the light softens, the mountains glow and Marrakchi families arrive to stroll, picnic among the olive trees and fish in the basin, giving the place a relaxed, authentically local atmosphere quite different from the tourist-heavy medina sights. You can climb into the pavilion for a small fee for the classic elevated view over the pool, and legend holds that a sultan would toss his overnight companions into the basin at dawn. There is not a great deal to 'do' here beyond soak up the tranquil setting, so it suits a gentle late-afternoon excursion rather than a major outing, ideally combined with a visit to the nearby Koutoubia or as a peaceful pause between other activities. Bring water, a camera and a little time to simply sit by the water as the sun goes down over the olives.",
