    fullDescription: "Taking a hammam is one of the most rewarding and authentically Moroccan things you can do in Marrakech, a centuries-old ritual of steam, cleansing and scrubbing that remains woven into daily and social life. The hammam is a public steam bath, traditionally attached to a mosque and sharing its furnace, where locals go weekly to wash, relax and socialise; the process involves acclimatising through a series of increasingly hot, humid rooms, being lathered in beldi (black olive-oil soap), and then vigorously scrubbed all over with a coarse kessa mitt that sloughs away astonishing quantities of dead skin, before a final rinse and, often, a rhassoul clay mask or argan-oil massage. Visitors have two very different options. A genuine neighbourhood hammam is cheap (a few dirhams for entry, a little more for an attendant to scrub you), communal, gender-segregated by time or space, and a wonderful, slightly daunting immersion in local life — bring your own soap, mitt, towel and flip-flops, and a sense of humour. At the other end, countless riads, dedicated spas and luxury hotels offer beautifully appointed private or semi-private tourist hammams with candlelight, marble, rose petals and full treatment packages of scrub, mask and massage for anywhere from 150 to several hundred dirhams — gentler, more pampering and much easier for the uninitiated. Either way, go with an open mind, stay hydrated, leave your modesty at the door (underwear is usually kept on), and emerge glowing, deeply relaxed and softer-skinned than you have ever been. It is the perfect restorative after long, dusty days pounding the souks and palaces.",
