    fullDescription: "Café Clock is far more than a place to eat — it is a cultural hub and one of the friendliest, most creative spaces in the medina, a sister to the original in Fez that has become a fixture of the Kasbah quarter near the Saadian Tombs. Spread over several floors and terraces of a converted building, it was founded to be a genuine meeting point between visitors and young Moroccans, and that mission runs through everything it does. The food is casual, affordable and international-meets-Moroccan — its signature is the much-Instagrammed camel burger, a surprisingly good and lean patty, alongside falafel, salads, mezze, cakes and proper coffee — but the real draw is the programme. There are regular sunset storytelling sessions on Thursdays, where a young Moroccan hakawati revives the ancient oral tradition of Jemaa el-Fnaa in English and Arabic; live Gnaoua and fusion music nights; and a roster of hands-on workshops in Moroccan cooking, calligraphy and Arabic that let you engage with the culture rather than just observe it. The atmosphere is relaxed, arty and welcoming, drawing a young, international and local crowd, and the rooftop is a pleasant spot for a drink or a light meal at any time of day. Prices are modest. While purists might quibble that a camel burger is hardly traditional, the whole ethos of cross-cultural exchange, storytelling and creativity makes Café Clock a genuinely enriching stop — a place to linger, meet people and get under the skin of the city rather than simply refuel.",
