    fullDescription: "Rue Mouassine and the surrounding Mouassine quarter represent a more curated, contemporary side of Marrakech shopping, and they have become the fashionable heart of the medina's design scene. Running through an elegant historic neighbourhood centred on the sixteenth-century Mouassine Mosque and its beautiful ornamental fountain, the street and its offshoots are lined not with the chaotic stalls of the trade souks but with a growing number of stylish concept stores, design boutiques, galleries and ateliers, many opened by Moroccan and European designers who have made the city their base. Here you will find beautifully sourced and often ethically made goods: contemporary takes on Moroccan textiles, rugs and babouches; well-designed homeware, ceramics and lighting; leather and fashion pieces with a modern sensibility; natural cosmetics; jewellery; and carefully edited selections of vintage and artisan-made objects. The key difference from the souks proper is the retail experience: prices are frequently fixed and clearly displayed, the quality and provenance are higher and more transparent, and browsing is pressure-free, making it a relaxing antidote to the hard bargaining elsewhere — though you will pay more for the curation and design credentials. It is the place to come for distinctive, high-quality pieces and gifts that feel current rather than merely touristy, and for anyone who finds the intensity of the main souks overwhelming. Interspersed with lovely cafés, riads and the odd museum (the restored Mouassine Museum sits nearby), the quarter also makes for a pleasant, refined wander. For design-minded shoppers, Rue Mouassine is arguably the most rewarding street in the medina.",
