    fullDescription: "The day trip to Aït Ben Haddou and Ouarzazate is one of the most spectacular you can make from Marrakech, combining an unforgettable mountain drive with one of Morocco's most iconic sights, though the distances mean it is a very long day and is arguably even better as an overnight. The route heads south over the High Atlas via the Tizi n'Tichka, at 2,260 metres the highest major road pass in the country, a thrilling, serpentine climb through Berber villages, terraced hillsides and dramatic switchbacks with viewpoints at every turn. On the far side lies the pre-Saharan landscape and the star of the trip: the ksar of Aït Ben Haddou, a magnificent fortified village of red mud-brick kasbahs and houses stacked up a hillside above a river, a UNESCO World Heritage Site and one of the finest surviving examples of southern Moroccan earthen architecture. Crossing the river (often on stepping stones) and climbing up through its labyrinth of alleys to the granary at the summit, with sweeping desert views, is magical, and film buffs will recognise it from Gladiator, Game of Thrones, Lawrence of Arabia and countless other productions. Trips usually also stop at nearby Ouarzazate, the 'door of the desert' and Morocco's film capital, home to the Atlas Film Studios and the imposing Taourirt Kasbah. The scenery throughout — palm-filled oasis valleys, rose-gold kasbahs, snow-capped peaks behind desert plains — is simply stunning. The catch is around four hours' driving each way, so this suits those who don't mind car time and want to see the epic landscapes beyond the mountains; going with a good driver-guide, or staying a night to press on toward the Dadès or the dunes, makes it far more relaxed and rewarding.",
