    fullDescription: "The Agafay Desert has boomed in popularity as the 'desert on Marrakech's doorstep', offering a taste of desert magic without the long haul to the Sahara's great dunes, and it makes an easy and atmospheric half-day or evening escape just forty-five minutes from the city. It is important to set expectations: Agafay is a stony, arid desert of rolling, lunar-like hills and dry valleys rather than a sea of golden sand dunes — the classic Saharan dunescape lies many hours further east at Merzouga or Zagora — but its stark, empty, undulating beauty, especially glowing in the low light of sunrise and sunset with the snow-capped Atlas often visible on the horizon, is genuinely striking and photogenic in its own right. What has made Agafay a phenomenon is the wave of desert camps and experiences that have sprung up across it, ranging from rustic to seriously luxurious, offering visitors a menu of quintessential desert activities within an easy drive: camel rides across the hills, quad-biking and buggy tours over the pistes, horse riding, and above all the hugely popular sunset-and-dinner packages, where you watch the sun go down over the desert before enjoying a Moroccan feast under a canopy of stars, often with live Gnaoua music, fire shows and, at the plusher camps, real comfort. Many people come specifically for that magical evening experience, and some stay overnight in the glamping tents for stargazing and a dawn view. Because it is so close and so flexible, Agafay is ideal for travellers who want the romance, adventure and photogenic drama of the desert but lack the time or appetite for the long journey to the true Sahara. Book a reputable camp or operator with transfers included.",
