    fullDescription: "The Ouzoud Falls are the most impressive waterfalls in Morocco and one of the most spectacular natural day trips from Marrakech, a magnificent series of cascades plunging some 110 metres in stages into a green, gorge-like river canyon in the Middle Atlas, around two and a half hours' drive north-east of the city. The name comes from the Berber word for olives, and the surrounding hillsides are indeed thick with old olive groves and small mills. Arriving at the top, you descend a shaded path lined with cafés and stalls down into the gorge, and the falls reveal themselves gradually and dramatically, the sheets of water throwing up clouds of spray in which rainbows constantly form, especially in the afternoon sun — a genuinely breathtaking sight, and a refreshingly cool, green one in a country of deserts and dry mountains. At the base, small, brightly painted boats ferry visitors close to the thundering water for a (splashy) up-close view, and there are natural pools where the hardy can swim. A special highlight, particularly for families, is the resident troop of wild Barbary macaques (a threatened species native to Morocco) that live in the olive and oak woods around the falls and can often be seen up close along the trails — enchanting to watch, though you should not feed or crowd them. The walk down and back up is moderately strenuous over uneven, sometimes slippery paths, so decent footwear is important, and it is worth allowing time for a leisurely tagine lunch at one of the terraced cafés overlooking the cascades. Beautiful, cooling, lively and full of natural drama, Ouzoud is a superb full-day escape into a greener, wilder side of Morocco.",
