    fullDescription: "El Jadida offers an appealing combination of beach and history that sets it apart from the coast's purely seaside resorts, making it a rewarding destination for those who like their sand with a side of culture. This mid-sized Atlantic city, north-west of Marrakech toward Casablanca, grew around a remarkable Portuguese fortress town: the Cité Portugaise, founded in the early sixteenth century as the fortified trading post of Mazagan and now a UNESCO World Heritage Site, is a compact, walled citadel of star-shaped ramparts, old churches, cisterns and Portuguese-Moroccan architecture that you can walk around and along, with fine sea views from the bastions. Its jewel is the extraordinary underground cistern, a vaulted, column-filled subterranean chamber whose still floor-water creates a perfect, ethereal mirror reflection of the arches and a single shaft of light from a central opening — a hauntingly beautiful, much-photographed space that famously featured in Orson Welles's film Othello. Beyond the historic core, El Jadida is a lively, workaday Moroccan city with a long stretch of wide sandy beach along its corniche, popular with local families and busy in summer, plus more beaches and the upscale Mazagan resort complex nearby. It is less geared to international tourists than Essaouira or Agadir, which lends it an authentic, everyday feel, and its amenities, restaurants and transport links (including train connections via Casablanca) are good. The combination of a genuinely fascinating historic monument, an atmospheric old town, and accessible city beaches makes El Jadida a solid choice for travellers wanting more than sun and sand, though at around three and a half hours from Marrakech it is best approached as an overnight or as part of a coastal itinerary rather than a day trip.",
