    fullDescription: "Safi is a large, gritty working port and industrial city on the Atlantic that will never top a conventional beach-holiday list, but it holds real interest for two particular kinds of traveller: surfers and craft enthusiasts. For the wave-riders, Safi is legendary: just north of the city lies one of the finest and fastest right-hand point breaks in the world, a powerful, barrelling wave that on its best days draws experienced surfers from across the globe and has hosted international competitions — strictly one for advanced surfers rather than beginners or swimmers, but a genuine bucket-list wave for those with the skill. For everyone else, Safi's great draw is pottery: the city has been Morocco's ceramics capital for centuries, and its hillside pottery quarter, the Colline des Potiers, is a fascinating warren of workshops and kilns where you can watch artisans throw, glaze and fire the distinctive blue-and-white and polychrome Safi ceramics by hand, and buy plates, bowls, tagines and tiles directly from the source at excellent prices. The old medina, the Portuguese-era Kechla fortress and the Dar el Bahar sea castle add further historic interest. The city beaches are functional and used by locals rather than scenic tourist strands, and the industrial fringes are unlovely, so Safi is really a stop for its surf, its pottery and its authentic, untouristy urban Moroccan character rather than for beach lounging. It lies around three and a half hours west of Marrakech and is most often visited as part of a wider coastal road trip taking in Oualidia and El Jadida to the north, or by dedicated surfers making the trip for that world-class wave. For craft lovers and hardcore surfers it rewards the detour; sun-seekers are better served elsewhere on the coast.",
