    fullDescription: "Marrakech operates the classic Moroccan two-tier taxi system, and understanding the difference between the two types saves money and confusion. The petit taxi is the workhorse of city travel: small, beige-coloured cars (each city has its own colour) that carry up to three passengers on short journeys within the greater urban area. They are cheap and plentiful, and by law they are metered — a typical hop across town costs only around 20–40 MAD — but, as at the airport, many drivers will try to avoid the meter and quote an inflated flat fare to tourists, so make a habit of politely but firmly asking them to switch on the compteur as you get in, or agree a sensible price beforehand; fares rise by around 50% after 8pm. Petits taxis can be hailed on the street, picked up at ranks (including at Jemaa el-Fnaa and the main gates), and are often shared, so a driver may pick up other passengers heading the same way. The grand taxi is a different beast: larger, older cream-coloured Mercedes sedans that run fixed longer-distance routes, both to outer suburbs and, importantly, to nearby towns and the mountains (the Ourika Valley, Imlil, Essaouira and so on). These operate as shared taxis, cramming up to six passengers in and leaving from designated stations when full, with a low fixed fare per seat, or you can negotiate to hire the whole car (a 'course') for a private trip or day excursion, which is a good-value way for a small group to reach the Atlas or a day-trip destination. Neither type is generally booked by phone; you find them at ranks and stations. For getting around the sprawling parts of the city the medina lanes don't reach, and for reaching the surrounding region on a budget, taxis are indispensable — just stay firm on the meter or a pre-agreed fare.",
