    fullDescription: "Marrakech has a city bus network operated by the company ALSA, and while it is extremely cheap, it is of fairly limited practical use to most short-stay visitors, who tend to rely on walking and petit taxis instead. The buses are useful mainly for a handful of specific journeys and for the budget-conscious. Standard city fares are minimal — only a few dirhams, paid to the driver or conductor on boarding — making it by far the cheapest way to move around, and the network does link the key zones a tourist might want, connecting the medina (with a major hub at Jemaa el-Fnaa) to the new town of Gueliz, the hotel district of Hivernage, the train station and the outer suburbs and residential areas. The single line most visitors actually use is the L19 airport shuttle bus, a purpose-run service on comfortable coaches that loops between Menara Airport, Jemaa el-Fnaa and the main new-town avenues roughly every half hour for around 30 MAD including a return valid for two weeks — a genuinely handy and economical airport link. Beyond that, the ordinary city buses have real drawbacks for tourists: they can be old, hot and very crowded, especially at peak times; routes and schedules are not always clearly signed or easy to decipher without local knowledge or an app like Google Maps; they are slow, snarled in the city's heavy traffic; and pickpocketing can be an issue in the crush. Given how walkable the car-free medina is and how cheap petits taxis are for the journeys it doesn't cover, most visitors find they rarely need the buses at all. For those on the tightest of budgets, or curious to travel as locals do, they are a viable option for longer cross-city hops, but for the majority the airport bus is the only line worth bothering with.",
