    fullDescription: "The calèche — a traditional open horse-drawn carriage — is one of Marrakech's most romantic and enduring forms of transport, and while it is squarely a tourist experience rather than a practical way to get around, it offers a genuinely pleasant and scenic way to take in the city's monuments and layout at a gentle, old-fashioned pace. These green four-wheeled carriages, a fixture of the city for well over a century, gather at ranks around Jemaa el-Fnaa, near the Koutoubia Mosque and outside the larger hotels, and their drivers offer set circuits that typically follow the long line of the medieval red ramparts that encircle the medina, passing through and pausing at the great historic gates (babs), rolling past the Koutoubia, the royal palace quarter and the Mellah, and often taking in the leafy avenues of the new town or the gardens of the Menara. It is an especially appealing way to travel with children, who love the horses and the elevated open-air ride, and a restful, shaded option for footsore visitors wanting an overview without walking. The fares are officially regulated at an hourly rate per carriage — not per person — of around 150 MAD, though rates can be higher for longer or negotiated tours, so the golden rule is to establish the price, the duration and the route clearly and agree them with the driver before you climb aboard, to avoid any dispute at the end. It is also worth choosing a carriage whose horse appears healthy, well-fed and properly cared for, as welfare standards vary. Best enjoyed in the softer light and cooler temperatures of the early morning or late afternoon rather than the midday heat, a calèche ride is a delightful, quintessentially Marrakech way to see the sights, particularly for families and first-time visitors.",
