    fullDescription: "Marrakech is well connected to the rest of Morocco by a reliable network of trains and intercity coaches, making it an easy base from which to explore further afield or a natural stop on a wider Moroccan itinerary. Trains are operated by the national railway, ONCF, from the handsome modern Marrakech station in the new town of Gueliz, which marks the current southern terminus of the country's rail network. From here comfortable, air-conditioned trains run frequently north to Casablanca (around three hours), continuing to the capital Rabat, and on to Fez, Meknes and Tangier, with first- and second-class carriages at very reasonable fares (Casablanca costs only around 100–140 MAD); it is worth reserving a seat in first class on busy routes and holidays. Note that the high-speed Al Boraq line currently runs only between Tangier and Casablanca, so the Marrakech leg is on standard, if perfectly pleasant, trains. Because the rail network does not extend south or west to popular destinations like Essaouira, Agadir, Ouarzazate and the desert, those journeys are made by road, and here the two premium intercity coach companies come into their own: CTM and Supratours (the latter run by ONCF) both operate modern, comfortable, air-conditioned buses from their own stations near the train station and city centre, with fixed schedules, assigned seats, luggage handling and online booking. Supratours coaches to Essaouira (around three hours, roughly 90 MAD) are a particularly popular and easy way to reach the coast, and both companies serve Agadir, the south and the main northern cities as an alternative to the train. These premium coaches are far more comfortable and reliable than the cheaper local bus operators and are the recommended choice for intercity travel where the train doesn't reach. Book ahead for popular routes and holiday periods.",
