    fullDescription: "Ride-hailing and car hire both play a limited but occasionally useful role in getting around Marrakech and its region, though neither is as central to the visitor experience as in some cities, and there are important local quirks to understand. Ride-hailing apps exist in a somewhat grey area in Marrakech: the big international name Uber pulled out of Morocco years ago, and the local taxi lobby has resisted app-based competition, but apps such as Careem, Roby and especially inDrive (where you propose a fare) do operate to varying degrees, effectively summoning ordinary drivers or taxis, often with pick-ups made a little discreetly away from official ranks. They can be handy for fixing a transparent price in advance and avoiding meter haggling, particularly for airport runs or trips across the sprawling new town, but coverage and reliability are patchy and the legal situation is ambiguous, so most visitors still find flagging a metered petit taxi the simplest option for city journeys. Hiring a car, meanwhile, makes little sense for exploring Marrakech itself — the historic medina is entirely car-free, parking in the city is difficult, and traffic, with its swarms of scooters, is chaotic and intimidating for the uninitiated, so a car is more liability than asset in town. Where self-drive comes into its own is for independent exploration of the wider region: having your own vehicle gives real freedom to tour the Atlas Mountains, the Ourika and other valleys, Essaouira and the coast, or to embark on a multi-day loop to Ouarzazate, the Dadès and the desert at your own pace. Rental is affordable, with international and local firms at the airport and in Gueliz, but be prepared for assertive local driving, frequent police checkpoints (keep documents handy and observe speed limits), and rough roads in the mountains. For most city-focused visitors, though, a hire car is best left until a day-trip or road-trip day, with taxis and walking covering everything else.",
