    fullDescription: "Marrakech is one of the best-value city-break destinations within easy reach of Europe, offering a huge range of experiences at a fraction of Western prices, though it also caters lavishly to those wanting to splurge. As a rough guide per person per day, excluding flights, a budget backpacker staying in a hostel dorm (around $15) or a cheap riad room, eating from the street stalls and cheap local cafés (a filling meal of tagine or brochettes for a few dollars, a bowl of harira soup for under a dollar), walking the free medina and using shared taxis and buses, can comfortably get by on around $25–40 a day. A mid-range traveller staying in a lovely boutique riad (perhaps $70–130 for a double with breakfast), enjoying a mix of atmospheric sit-down restaurants and street food, paying entry to a few palaces and gardens, taking the odd taxi and treating themselves to a spa hammam, should budget roughly $70–130 a day. A luxury trip, with a grand palace hotel or high-end riad, fine dining, private guides and drivers, and desert excursions, can run to $300 a day and far beyond — the sky is genuinely the limit at the top end. The great advantage for the budget-minded is how much of Marrakech's magic is cheap or free: wandering the souks and medina, watching the nightly spectacle on Jemaa el-Fnaa, browsing the markets and soaking up the atmosphere cost nothing, while food, taxis, entry fees and local hammams are all inexpensive. The biggest variables are your choice of accommodation, how much you shop (and how well you haggle), and whether you book pricey organised excursions. With sensible spending and sharp bargaining, Marrakech delivers extraordinary richness of experience for very little money.",
