    fullDescription: "Eating in Marrakech can be astonishingly cheap or genuinely expensive depending on where you go, giving visitors great flexibility. At the budget end, the city excels in wonderful-value street and local food: a bowl of harira (the hearty tomato, lentil and chickpea soup) costs well under a dollar, a plate of grilled brochettes or a tagine from a busy stall or hole-in-the-wall café runs 30–60 MAD, a fresh khobz sandwich or a portion of mechoui lamb is similarly cheap, and the legendary fresh orange juice on Jemaa el-Fnaa is just a few dirhams a glass. Eating at the nightly food stalls on the great square, or at simple local eateries away from the tourist drag, you can fill up handsomely for a handful of dollars. Mid-range restaurants aimed at visitors — the atmospheric riad rooftops and courtyard dining rooms — charge more, with mains typically 80–150 MAD and a full meal perhaps 200–350 MAD a head, still very reasonable by European standards for the lovely settings. At the top, the grand palace-restaurant banquets like Dar Yacout or an evening at Le Tobsil run 600–800 MAD per person for a lavish multi-course feast, often including wine. The single biggest factor pushing up a bill is alcohol: Marrakech is a Muslim city where drink is served only in licensed venues — smart restaurants, hotels, riads and rooftop bars, largely in the new town and on medina rooftops — and where it is available, imported wine, beer and cocktails carry a hefty premium, so a few drinks can easily cost more than the food. Tap water is not recommended, so budget for bottled water. Tipping is customary but modest: rounding up or leaving 5–10% for good service in restaurants, and a few dirhams for café staff, porters and guides, is appreciated and expected. Overall, careful eaters feast for very little, while those wanting wine and smart surroundings should budget considerably more.",
